Enabling the SCIP Rust backend makes indexing execute the analysed crate's
build.rs and proc macros as the invoking user. Until now the only way to make
that choice STICK was exporting HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 from a shell
profile — which is GLOBAL, so it opted in every Rust repository the user would
ever analyse, including ones cloned specifically to audit (WI-sobig). The
decision is per-repository; the only persistence available had the wrong scope
for it.

Grants now live in $XDG_STATE_HOME/hypergumbo/trust.d, mode 0600, OUTSIDE
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME. That separation is the point and a test asserts the two do
not nest: a config directory is designed to be synced between machines and
committed to dotfiles repos, and a synced grant of code execution is the same
footgun by a nicer route.

KEYED BY RESOLVED ABSOLUTE PATH, deliberately NOT by ADR-0013's
repo-fingerprint. That key (remote URL + first commit SHA) exists so multiple
checkouts SHARE a cache — right for a cache, exactly wrong here: two clones of
one remote can have different working trees, and it is the tree whose build.rs
runs. Paths resolve through symlinks so one tree cannot acquire two divergent
decisions.

A DECLINE IS A DECISION, so the nudge stops asking. read_decision returns None
for "never asked" and granted=False for "asked and said no" — the same
three-valued discipline the opt-in resolver uses.

